# Schema Documentation for GraphDB Projections
#
# This file contains only high‑level documentation about the RDF types that are
# projected from the Python contracts in `contracts/inference_contracts`.
# It is intentionally *not* a full OWL ontology – the actual triples are
# generated at runtime by the projection code in `backbone-api/projections/graphdb`.
#
# The two core types are:
#   * `ex:Claim` – a statement produced by the validator‑agent after an LLM
#     has processed a human task and a worker‑agent response.
#   * `ex:Evidence` – the tool‑call records that the worker‑agent produced
#     while the LLM was issuing callbacks.
#
# Each type is represented as an RDF class with a set of object and datatype
# properties that mirror the fields of the corresponding Python dataclass.
# The projection code builds a Turtle document for each instance and POSTs it
# to GraphDB via the client in `clients/graphdb_client.py`.
#
# The following sections describe the intent of each property.  The actual
# property URIs are defined in the `EX` namespace (http://mindbodyengineer.com/).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Claim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ex:hasClaimId          – unique identifier for the claim.
#   ex:hasClaimNumber      – human‑readable claim number.
#   ex:hasImportance       – numeric importance score.
#   ex:hasExecutionId      – ID of the LLM execution that produced the claim.
#   ex:hasContent          – the textual claim.
#   ex:hasContentHash      – hash of the content for deduplication.
#   ex:observedAt          – timestamp when the claim was observed.
#   ex:retrievedAt         – timestamp when the claim was retrieved from the LLM.
#   ex:hasEmbeddingModel   – name of the embedding model used.
#   ex:hasEmbeddingTask    – task identifier for the embedding.
#   ex:hasValidatorVersion – version of the validator that produced the claim.
#   ex:hasSchemaVersion    – version of the claim schema.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Evidence
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ex:hasEvidenceId       – unique identifier for the evidence.
#   ex:hasExecutionId      – ID of the worker execution that produced the evidence.
#   ex:hasEventId          – ID of the event that triggered the evidence.
#   ex:hasEvidenceType     – type of evidence (e.g., "tool_call", "log", ...).
#   ex:hasContent          – textual content of the evidence.
#   ex:hasContentHash      – hash of the content.
#   ex:hasSourceType       – type of source (e.g., "tool", "LLM", ...).
#   ex:hasSourceName       – human‑readable name of the source.
#   ex:hasSourceUrl        – URL of the source, if applicable.
#   ex:observedAt          – timestamp when the evidence was observed.
#   ex:retrievedAt         – timestamp when the evidence was retrieved.
#   ex:hasExtractionMethod – method used to extract the evidence.
#   ex:hasParentEvidence   – object property pointing to other Evidence nodes.
#   ex:hasWorkerVersion    – version of the worker that produced the evidence.
#   ex:hasSchemaVersion    – version of the evidence schema.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# * All timestamps are stored as ISO‑8601 strings.
# * Hashes are stored as plain strings; the projection code ensures they are
#   present before serialisation.
# * The `ex:hasParentEvidence` property is used to model a chain of evidence
#   (e.g., a tool call that produced another tool call).
# * No additional OWL restrictions or property chains are defined here – the
#   projection logic is intentionally simple and stateless.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# End of documentation.
